    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/creoda-of-mercia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/creoda-of-mercia.jpg" alt="Creoda of Mercia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/creoda-of-mercia">Creoda of Mercia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Creda_-_John_Speed.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Speed, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mercia, from the Old English <em>Mierce</em>, the 'border people', took shape among Anglian settlers in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> valley, around what later became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire">Staffordshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbyshire">Derbyshire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicestershire">Leicestershire</a>. Its beginnings are obscure. Later royal genealogies trace the kings back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/icel">Icel</a> and name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/creoda-of-mercia">Creoda</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pybba-of-mercia">Pybba</a> as early rulers in the late 6th century, and the traditional starting date of 527 rests on such late sources rather than on contemporary record. The first Mercian king named by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cearl-of-mercia">Cearl</a>, whose daughter married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edwin-of-northumbria">Edwin of Northumbria</a> in the early 7th century. Mercia emerged as a real power under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda</a> (d. 655), a pagan warlord who probably became king in the 620s or 630s. In alliance with the British king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadwallon-ap-cadfan">Cadwallon of Gwynedd</a> he defeated and killed Edwin at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hatfield-chase">Battle of Hatfield Chase</a> in 633, and in 642 he killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswald-of-northumbria">Oswald of Northumbria</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maserfield">Maserfield</a>. Penda also killed several kings of East Anglia and set his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peada">Peada</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-angles">Middle Angles</a>. By absorbing smaller peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a>, such as the Middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a>, the Hwicce around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a> and the Magonsaete on the Welsh border, Mercia grew from a frontier people into the largest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kingdoms.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/trento"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trento.jpg" alt="Trento" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trento</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trento_visione_d%27insieme_6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BelPatty86, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mercia was a land of mixed farming in the river valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-severn">Severn</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-avon-warwickshire">Avon</a>, organised around royal estates that collected food renders from the surrounding countryside. A document known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tribal-hidage">Tribal Hidage</a>, probably drawn up for a Mercian overlord in the 7th or 8th century, assesses the peoples of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in hides, the units of land that supported a household and owed dues to the king. Society was led by the king and a warrior aristocracy bound to him by gifts of land and treasure; beneath them were free farmers and a large unfree population. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire-hoard">Staffordshire Hoard</a>, found near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a> in 2009, contains several thousand pieces of gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> war gear from the 7th century and shows the wealth that circulated among Mercian warriors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> arrived after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda</a>'s death through Irish-trained missionaries from Northumbria; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> established the see of Lichfield in 669, and monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repton">Repton</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterborough-abbey">Medeshamstede</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterborough">Peterborough</a>) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breedon-on-the-hill">Breedon</a> flourished. Control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, the leading port of southern England, gave the kings access to trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a>, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa</a> a broad, carefully designed silver penny became the standard coin of much of England.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hatfield-chase"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-hatfield-chase.jpg" alt="Battle of Hatfield Chase" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hatfield-chase">Battle of Hatfield Chase</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Death_of_Edwin_of_Northumbria_at_Hatfield_-_F._H._Townsend.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">F. H. Townsend, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mercian warfare was the warfare of a royal war band, the king's sworn companions reinforced by levies from client peoples and allies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda</a>'s victories at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hatfield-chase">Hatfield Chase</a> in 633 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maserfield">Maserfield</a> in 642 made Mercia the dominant power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a>, but his defeat and death at the hands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiu">Oswiu of Northumbria</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-winwaed">Battle of the Winwaed</a> in 655 led to three years of Northumbrian occupation. A rising of Mercian nobles in 658 placed Penda's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wulfhere-of-mercia">Wulfhere</a> on the throne, and his brother Æthelred defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecgfrith-of-northumbria">Ecgfrith of Northumbria</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-trent">Battle of the Trent</a> in 679, ending Northumbrian claims south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a> and recovering Lindsey. In the 8th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelbald-of-mercia">Æthelbald</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa</a> fought the Welsh kingdoms and compelled the southern kingdoms to accept their lordship. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offas-dyke">Offa's Dyke</a>, an earthwork bank and ditch running for much of the Welsh border, was attributed to Offa by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asser">Asser</a> in the 890s and is the largest earthwork of early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Mercian kings also imposed obligations of bridge and fortress building on their lands. The army was broken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egbert-of-wessex">Ecgberht of Wessex</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ellendun">Battle of Ellendun</a> in 825 and could not withstand the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a>, which occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repton">Repton</a> in 873–874.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelbald-of-mercia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aethelbald-of-mercia.jpg" alt="Æthelbald of Mercia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelbald-of-mercia">Æthelbald of Mercia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Repton_Stone-_detail.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Poliphilo, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mercian kings ruled through itinerant courts that moved between royal estates such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamworth">Tamworth</a>, their principal residence, and the church centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repton">Repton</a>, where several kings were buried. Royal succession was open to any male of royal descent, and in the 8th and 9th centuries the throne passed between rival kindreds, often violently: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelbald-of-mercia">Æthelbald</a> was murdered by his own bodyguard at Seckington in 757. Mercian power rested on overlordship. Client kings of the Hwicce, the Magonsaete and Lindsey were gradually reduced to ealdormen, and kings of Kent, Sussex and East Anglia either submitted or were removed. Æthelbald was styled 'king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>' in a charter of 736. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa</a> (757–796) went further, annexing Kent and Sussex, having <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelberht-ii-of-east-anglia">Æthelberht II of East Anglia</a> beheaded in 794 and persuading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> to create an archbishopric at Lichfield in 787, which lasted until 803. In 787 he also had his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecgfrith-of-mercia">Ecgfrith</a> consecrated as king, the first such anointing recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Councils of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and nobles, often held at a place called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clofesho">Clofesho</a>, issued charters and settled church affairs. Offa's legislation is mentioned by Alfred, though its text does not survive.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/all-saints-church-brixworth"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/all-saints-church-brixworth.jpg" alt="All Saints' Church, Brixworth" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/all-saints-church-brixworth">All Saints' Church, Brixworth</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:All_Saints%27_Church,_Brixworth_89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Simon Burchell, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For most of the 8th century Mercia was the leading kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and historians speak of a 'Mercian supremacy' under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelbald-of-mercia">Æthelbald</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa</a>. Offa's correspondence with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, including a letter of 796 concerning trade and the protection of merchants and pilgrims, placed an English king in diplomatic exchange with the greatest ruler of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> penny set a pattern of coinage that English kings followed for centuries, and a gold coin imitating an Abbasid dinar with the added words OFFA REX shows the reach of contemporary trade. Mercian churches and scriptoria produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and sculpture of high quality, including the carvings at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breedon-on-the-hill">Breedon on the Hill</a> and the church at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-saints-church-brixworth">Brixworth</a>, and Mercian scholars mattered to the later English revival: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> recruited four Mercian clerics, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waerferth-of-worcester">Wærferth of Worcester</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plegmund">Plegmund</a>, later archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, to help with his programme of learning. The Mercian dialect of Old English shaped later written English. The name survived the kingdom in the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/earl-of-mercia">Earldom of Mercia</a> of the 11th century, held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leofric-earl-of-mercia">Leofric</a> and his family, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offas-dyke">Offa's Dyke</a> remains one of the best-known monuments of early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ellendun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ellendun.jpg" alt="Battle of Ellendun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ellendun">Battle of Ellendun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ellandun.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Derville Rowlandson, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mercian dominance collapsed within a generation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa</a>'s death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coenwulf-of-mercia">Coenwulf</a> (796–821) held much of it together and crushed a revolt in Kent in 798, but disputed successions followed, and in 825 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egbert-of-wessex">Ecgberht of Wessex</a> defeated King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beornwulf-of-mercia">Beornwulf</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ellendun">Battle of Ellendun</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> took over Kent, Surrey, Sussex and Essex, East Anglia broke free, and in 829 Ecgberht briefly ruled Mercia itself before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiglaf-of-mercia">Wiglaf</a> recovered it in 830. In the following decades Mercia cooperated with Wessex, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgred-of-mercia">Burgred</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelswith">Æthelswith</a>, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> wintered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottingham">Nottingham</a> in 867–868 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repton">Repton</a> in 873–874, forcing Burgred to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The Vikings installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceolwulf-ii-of-mercia">Ceolwulf II</a> and in 877 divided the kingdom, settling the eastern half, later part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a>, and leaving the west to him. The western half was then ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-lord-of-the-mercians">Æthelred, Lord of the Mercians</a>, who accepted Alfred's overlordship and married Alfred's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelflaed">Æthelflæd</a>. After his death in 911 Æthelflæd ruled alone, built a chain of fortified burhs and recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epsom-derby">Derby</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a>. When she died in June 918, her brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a> deposed her daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelfwynn">Ælfwynn</a> and took Mercia into the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> kingdom.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Mercia, the midland kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa</a>, was the dominant power of 8th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, issuing the penny that became England's standard coin, raising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offas-dyke">Offa's Dyke</a> and dealing with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> divided it and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelflaed">Æthelflæd</a>'s western Mercia merged into the emerging English kingdom.</em></p>

